    "turn_text": "Yeah, it's actually still mind boggling to me when I watch a Halloween class. So I mean, that certainly goes up to its mythology, the hell week and generally the training. That it's probably worse than you think, because you can only describe it and show it to people so much. When you're in it, it's unbelievably intense and difficult. That's why I say there's no way you'll make it through unless you just you truly gave yourself no other choice. I wrote a book called Fortitude. You can buy it on Amazon still. It's a great book. Bestseller. And it's one of the chapters in there is No Plan B. And what I mean by that is you should always plan. But No Plan B is a mindset. You know, it's a it's a mindset like I'm not going to give into my weaker self, whatever you're doing. Maybe it's like, maybe it's a project you're working on. Maybe it's buds, but plan B is taking shortcuts and on the projects you can go drink with your friends knowing full well you could have done better. It's the same with going through buds. If you have it in your head that like, I may quit. I mean, I'm going to do my best, but like I might not make it. You're going to quit. That's that mindset is you're done. You're only the only mindset that will get you through that is I will die before I quit. There is zero chance of me quitting. I might break my leg. at which point I have to kind of restore, you know, heal and restart. Because they ask you a question when I, especially when I broke my leg, it was during one of what we call a child runs, because you have to run the boats on everywhere you go through how weak it's got the boats on your head. So it's pretty bad on your spine and bones in general. On that shower, my leg had been bad throughout the first phase, the first three weeks, the first phase, fourth week is how weak. And it was just getting worse and worse and bad tendinitis and issues. So the more that your muscles fail and your tendons fail, the more stress goes on your bones and eventually just snap. Not like a compound fracture, but like a fracture. And I could feel it, kept running through the adrenaline, sat down for chow and then couldn't get back up. And the question that instructors ask is, are you hurt or are you injured? Because if you're hurt, well, and you stop, well, you're never coming back. But if you're injured, that's different. And we'll do an MRI, we'll do an X-ray, and we'll see if you're injured. Then you can come back, but that's an important distinction.",
