    "turn_text": "It was the one day we spent in Hellman Province. Our operational area was Kandahar Province, just the East of Hellman, in a sandwich between Hellman and Pakistan. And we were tasked with partnering with one of the more elites, you can call them that, but the more elite Afghan units called the commando units. And so our job is to take them throughout the province and You know, as I guess in military terms, you know, affect that red space, turn it into white space. You know, try to support local leaders that are counter Taliban. I think it's a counterinsurgency methodology. And so you get to know those areas pretty well, but for this for this particular op, we were tasked with at least maintaining presence in this bad area and saying in the saying in district of Hellman role. IEDs are everywhere, just absolutely everywhere. And in, and they're not just in doorsteps and pathways, they're just everywhere. And you're getting shot at, you're like, what's your first reaction to run for cover? Well, not there. You're better off just getting down instead of trying to run to cover and potentially hitting an IED. And so our tactics have to move accordingly. We don't. You know, you can't do your typical maneuvering in a place like this because you need to walk in a straight line, only in a straight line, where the path has been cleared. And, you know, we were taking on a kind of an empty compound there. Early in the",
