    "turn_text": "thought I was blind, not until waking up five days later being told that, but I just thought there was dirt in my eyes and everything was fine. I knew I'd been hit. I immediately felt my legs, you know, reach down and touch, make sure, okay, so I didn't step on it. It's the conclusion I reached and I could, I could hear Rock Mountain, our interpreter, And I knew, and I pretty much put it together where it had happened and kind of just stuck it out for the next 45 minutes while I waited for Medevac. And I was able to actually get myself up and go. And then I was pretty much immediately put into a medically induced coma for the next. Still not sure, maybe three to five days when they woke me up in landstool Germany. And the hell really started from there. I mean, that was a pretty hellish couple of weeks. Just can't move your bodies. I mean, it's like I got shot with a dozen shotguns. That's kind of how to describe what my body looks like. You know, the eyes were just the more permanent damage, but everything else was to shot up like you'd been shot with bird shot. Um, like he'd been, you know, it was just, and then also had Tabasco sauce portal over you. I think that's the best way to describe getting blown up. Um, and then, and then, and then just praying and hoping for surgery to go well. Cause the, my right eye was, was gone. They've removed that pretty immediately in Kandahar hospital actually. Cause you want to remove an eye. Um, if it's so badly damaged, there's no point in saving it. Cause you don't want your body to try and heal it. You want your body to try and heal the other one. And so not on the doctor's not allowed to have a lot of hope that I would ever see out of this eye. Well, again, I mean, I could see some light. It was all screwed up. My lens was destroyed. My iris is still destroyed. I was in my retina detached just a few years ago from the scarring. But the surgery did go well. And miraculously, I have correctable vision. If I 