  {
    "episode_id": 90392,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90392.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 113,
    "turn_text": "You talk about that being rational. First of all, my experience has been when people actually talk to each other, if they're actually talking to each other, it's much harder to hate people or caricatureize them. if you have a real conversation, which are discouraged and that actually leads me to another point, you've been pretty critical of the social media world and the social media in the world that it's created. I don't want to skip over your race for Congress because it was kind of extraordinary or an unknown and ran across your hundred miles across your district and ran the classic insurgent campaign and won, which was quite a feat. The, you know, our politics has basically now been, it feels like in many ways captured by social media, all its perverse profit motives, right? You know, outrage, resentment, conspiracy theories. You know, that's what keeps people online and it also helps you raise money. if you're a politician, and you've been sort of outspoken about that, and you've taken on some of your more colorful examples of that in your own caucus, Marjorie Taylor Greene, for example, who raises a shitload of money by being outrageous.",
