    "turn_idx": 173,
    "turn_text": "So first of all, I wouldn't be so sure that Trump and Vance are on the same page here. Vance is very clearly against Ukraine aid. And I am very clearly in disagreement with that. Trump has never said that. Trump has walked a very interestingly fine line. If you take really close attention to how, of course, you got to know Trump. And then you got to look at how he's talked about this. I don't believe that he has an intention of abandoning Ukraine. I'm not sure because he knows what he's going to do. But his history, because we've got four years of foreign policy to go off of, he was pretty strong on Ukraine as presidents. He made threats to Putin about, if you invade, we're going to bomb the show of a Moscow. It's famously quoted line. I'm not sure if it's true or not, but it's often quoted and Trump's never denied it. I believe that he said it. So deterrence is such an important concept. And Trump did inherently understand that. If you're going to do strength through peace, you got to actually have the strength part. And so my criticism of the anti-Ukraine aid folks is you're removing our deterrence for 50, I don't know, forever. because you're making it very clear that we just will not stand with an ally and that it's okay to engage in wars of conquest. And if you greenlight this idea that it's okay to engage in wars of conquest, which hasn't really happened since World War II, then you're setting yourself up for a pre-World War II war, or world. That's not good. Life wasn't good before World War II. We didn't have space travel. We didn't have, you know, Amazon delivering us everything we wanted in five seconds. We didn't have these incredibly complex and but now matured supply chains that really uplift the entire world and especially Americans in an enormous way. And there hasn't been enough education for people. I'm like, why do you have, you can complain about a lot of things. It's popular to complain. That's sort of the point of politics in some ways, I guess, but you still have it better than everyone else had it forever. Okay, especially if you're living in the United States. There's a reason for that. And the reason is largely because of American leadership in the world where we're just like, hey, we're going to be the most powerful and we're not going to let everybody just fight all the time. And we're going to enforce global trade and we're going to benefit from that. And, you know, this kind of populist movement that says we haven't benefited from it. I mean, they're It's obviously true that certain areas get hurt by global trade, and that's an issue that needs to be addressed. But if you throw the baby out with the bathwater, you're really gonna screw everybody. And global economics gets seriously upended when there's global war. And there's global war when dictators and tyrants like Putin or Xi Jinping realize that nobody will stop them if they just take what they want by force. It has major, major implications for your kid's future. And so for 5 to 10% of our defense spending, just letting somebody else fight the war for is not a bad investment. The other thing I always tell people is, you're fine. There's a lot of excuses made. Also, no, I walk Ukraine to win, but I want to see a better strategy. I want to help me too. I think Biden screwed up a lot of things there by slow walking, a lot of the aid. You should have been stronger from the beginning. And you wouldn't be in this situation right now where you've got a stalemate. But in the end, a stalemate hurts Russia, hell of a lot more than it'll ever hurt us. And because what's the goal? What are our missions? What's our good people ask that? Like they can't figure it out for themselves. And I'm like, the goals are very simple. Make Russia regret it. Make sure there's no more wars of conquest. It's that simple. And there used to be better tactics and strategies to get there quicker. But in the end, that's the cards were dealt. They're like, well, why can't we advocate for peace? You're just getting Ukrainians killed. I hear that one sometime. I hate to remind, that's a nice fantastical view of the world where if you just ask for peace, it happens. What a nice thought. Gotta write a fucking fiction novel about it. But in the real world, people like Putin have a say and they have no intention of stopping. And I would remind everybody the counterfactual. Counterfactuals are really important if you're going to assess foreign policy. The counterfactual here is pretty simple. Never support Ukraine. Don't give them sting or missiles. Don't give them javelins, which is what prevented those Russian tanks from getting into Kiev in those initial days. Don't give them any of that. And then what's the counterfactual? Russia plows through Ukraine, no problem. And now they're on the border of four more NATO countries. And guess what? Putin's also under the impression that we don't support allies. So you think he doesn't have intentions with the Baltic states? Just listen to him. He does. There's ethnic Russians speaking people there. He believes those are his very Hilarian language and a lot of this too. It's like, well, we need to expand our security permits. We need to save people who are like us. You think they don't have an interest in building a land bridge between Russia and Kaliningrad and the politics? Of course they do. So it's your Got to explain to people one how it is. This is the problem. Biden's never done this. Biden has never and Kamala now has never said anything close to what I just said. In fact, no one for the administration has said that, which is very frustrating. There's zero leadership for zero people up there having a frank discussion with the American people about why we need to do what we need to do. and selling them two things. One, this actually is in your interest. It's definitely in your kid's interest because you want America to still be the powerhouse in the world. When you let, when you don't call it America first when you're letting China and Russia just do what they want around the world. That's America last. That's the first thing. The second thing is it's more likely if you let Russia win and Ukraine, it's much more likely you actually get us into a shooting war because of what I just described. You're on the border for more NATO countries and you've been put in the green light that we don't support our allies. That has the potential for a massive, massive miscommunication between us and the Russians.",
