    "turn_text": "No doubt about it, but it's also true. I remember a focus group. You'll appreciate this that we did. for my institute of politics at the University of Chicago where I hope you'll come. But it was with a group of Trump voters in Peoria. And they were very concerned about immigration. This was a few years ago. And the moderator said, do you know anybody who's hired illegal? immigrants, and like everybody raised their hand. And one guy who was the most vociferous on the subject said, well, I do. And he said, really? Why do you? He said, well, they're the hardest working people around. And so it was kind of interesting. When you say we're going to engage in mass deportations, It's, I don't know if people are calculating at that point and that includes all the people who you rely on and all the people who you go to church with and all the people who, you know, man, the restaurants and other businesses that you, you know what I mean? I think it's more complicated than that. It's a complicated problem. We need, I believe we need a comprehensive solution that for sure includes Real enforceable border security. I mean, that's but as you say we're a long way from that kind of a solution That leads me to my last thing congressman You've worked with Democrats on issues you You're one of the people in Congress who? Looks for opportunities to work across the aisle Can we get back to? a place where you say there are legislators and there are performance artists. Can we make the world safe for legislators?",
